            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> * Whilst not the best example, it would auto jump if I opened a grep/file picker in a repo with only a single file. You get the point, not desirable for all pickers.\n\nTo be fair, I don't see a sufficient difference between \"jump to a file if it is the only one listed\" and \"jump to a definition if it is the only one listed\". On top of that,  the workaround from the above linked comment can be applied to a single picker by creating a wrapper for a specific picker (like `pickers.lsp` with `scope='definition'`).\n  \n> * LSP jumps on single result is an established behavior: JetBrains, VS Code, and even `vim.lsp.buf.definition()` do it.\n\nThe fact that `vim.lsp.buf.definition()` behaves that way is one of the reasons why `MiniExtra.pickers.lsp` doesn't have this functionality. Enabling 'mini.pick' makes it override `vim.ui.select` which is used as a part of `vim.lsp.buf.definition()`. So if users prefer the behavior of \"jump directly if there is a single definition\", my suggestion is to use `vim.lsp.buf.definition()` directly. That is what I use.\n\nA design of \"calling a function to open a picker should always open a picker\" is something that will be hard to argue against.\n\n\n\n",
