            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Thanks again, this would definitely be my preferred solution! I gave it a shot and while single results jump directly as expected, multiple results still route to the quickfix list rather than mini.pick. `vim.ui.select == MiniPick.ui_select` returns true so it seems like the integration is set up correctly. Could there be something else at play here?\n\nOh, sorry, my bad. I've mixed it up with `vim.lsp.buf.code_action()`. Yes, `vim.lsp.buf.definition()` by default indeed doesn't call `vim.ui.select()`, but populates quickfix.\n\nThen indeed there has to be some adjustments in order to have the combined behavior with 'mini.pick'. Either by providing custom `opts.on_list` for `vim.lsp.buf.definition()` (which is essentially what `MiniExtra.pickers.lsp` does) or by autochoosing a single item. With something like this:\n\n```lua\nlocal choose_single_item = function()\n  local items = MiniPick.get_picker_items()\n  if items == nil or #items ~= 1 then return end\n  local choose = MiniPick.get_picker_opts().source.choose or MiniPick.default_choose\n  MiniPick.stop()\n  choose(items[1])\nend\n\n-- This makes it possible to have `:Pick lsp_definition` command\nMiniPick.registry.lsp_definition = function()\n  vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('User', { pattern = 'MiniPickStart', once = true, callback = choose_single_item })\n  MiniExtra.pickers.lsp({ scope = 'definition' })\nend\n```",
